---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const canonicalURL = new URL('/resources/signs-your-business-outgrew-spreadsheets', site.url).href;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="5 Signs Your Business Has Outgrown Spreadsheets"
  description="Still running your books in spreadsheets? Here are 5 clear signs your business has outgrown them — and what to do about it before it costs you."
  canonicalURL={canonicalURL}
>

  <!-- ── Page Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2s1 page-hero" aria-label="5 Signs Your Business Has Outgrown Spreadsheets">
    <div class="b4s2s1-inner">
      <span class="label">Business</span>
      <h1>5 Signs Your Business Has Outgrown Spreadsheets</h1>
      <p class="hero-meta">
        By <strong>Accountium Team</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; Updated <strong>July 2026</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; &#9201; <strong>8 min read</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Spreadsheets are a great way to start. They're free, flexible, and familiar. But at some
        point, the same tool that helped you get off the ground starts holding you back. Here's
        how to tell when that point has arrived — and what to do about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most small businesses start with a spreadsheet. It's the natural first step — no setup,
        no cost, and total control. But as a business adds customers, employees, and transactions,
        that same spreadsheet quietly turns from a convenience into a liability. The tricky part
        is that it rarely happens all at once. It creeps up, one broken formula and one missed
        invoice at a time.
      </p>

      <!-- ── Real-Time & Audit Trail graphic ── -->
      <div class="rt-embed">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 360" role="img" aria-label="Accountium — same weekend, two different Mondays: a spreadsheet ends in a mismatch, Accountium ends in sync">
