  <!-- ── Sign 1 Divider ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="sign-1-heading">
    <h2 id="sign-1-heading">1. You're Spending Hours Reconciling, Not Reviewing</h2>
    <p>
      In the beginning, updating a spreadsheet takes minutes. Over time, as the number of tabs,
      formulas, and manual entries grows, so does the time it takes to make sure everything
      still adds up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 1 Content ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section bg-subtle" aria-label="Sign 1 detail">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <h3 class="feature-block-heading">Checking Numbers Instead of Acting on Them</h3>
        <p class="feature-block-body">
          A single mistyped number or a formula that didn't copy down correctly can throw off
          an entire report — and finding it can take longer than doing the original work.
          This is one of the most common signs of outgrowing spreadsheets: your week starts
          to revolve around <strong>checking</strong> your numbers instead of
          <strong>acting</strong> on them.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout--tip">
          <strong>Quick self-check</strong>
          How much of your bookkeeping time last month was spent fixing errors or tracing down
          a number that didn't match — versus actually reviewing what the numbers were telling you?
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 2 Divider ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="sign-2-heading">
    <h2 id="sign-2-heading">2. Multiple People Need Access — and Things Break</h2>
    <p>
      A spreadsheet works well when one person owns it. It gets risky the moment a second or
      third person needs to open it — a bookkeeper, a co-founder, an office manager.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 2 Content ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section" aria-label="Sign 2 detail">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <h3 class="feature-block-heading">No Audit Trail, No Version Control</h3>
        <p class="feature-block-body">
          Shared spreadsheets are prone to overwritten cells, accidentally deleted rows, and
          version confusion ("Budget_FINAL_v2_reallyfinal.xlsx" is a familiar sight). There's
          also no real audit trail — if a number changes, it's often unclear who changed it,
          when, or why.
        </p>
        <ul class="criteria-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Overwritten data</strong>
            <p>A bookkeeper updates payroll figures on Friday. Over the weekend, a co-founder opens the same file and accidentally saves over part of it. By Monday, nobody notices until the totals don't match the bank statement.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No permissions or roles</strong>
            <p>Everyone with the file has full edit access. There's no way to limit who can change what — which becomes a real problem for loan applications, investor conversations, or tax filings.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Version confusion</strong>
            <p>Which version is the source of truth? Without a shared system, teams end up maintaining multiple copies that quietly diverge over time.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 3 Divider ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="sign-3-heading">
    <h2 id="sign-3-heading">3. You Can't Get a Real-Time Picture of Cash Flow</h2>
    <p>
      A spreadsheet is a snapshot, not a live view. By the time it's updated with the latest
      invoices, payments, and expenses, it's already slightly out of date.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 3 Content ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section bg-subtle" aria-label="Sign 3 detail">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <h3 class="feature-block-heading">Making Decisions on Last Week's Numbers</h3>
        <p class="feature-block-body">
          For a very small operation, that lag might not matter. As transaction volume grows,
          it becomes a real blind spot — you're making decisions about hiring, spending, or a
          new contract based on numbers that were accurate last week, not today. This is
          especially risky for cash flow specifically, since it's the number that changes
          fastest and matters most when things get tight.
        </p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Hiring decisions based on stale data</li>
          <li>Missed payments due to invisible cash shortfalls</li>
          <li>Overcommitting on new contracts or suppliers</li>
          <li>No early warning when a slow month is coming</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 4 Divider ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="sign-4-heading">
    <h2 id="sign-4-heading">4. Tax Season Becomes a Fire Drill</h2>
    <p>
      If preparing for tax season means blocking off several days to manually pull totals,
      cross-check categories, and reconstruct records from scattered files, that's a strong signal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 4 Content ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section" aria-label="Sign 4 detail">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <h3 class="feature-block-heading">Categorization Has to Happen All at Once</h3>
        <p class="feature-block-body">
          Spreadsheets don't enforce consistent categorization, which means the work of making
          everything "tax ready" has to happen all at once, under a deadline, instead of
          continuously throughout the year. This is particularly costly for Canadian businesses
          managing GST/HST filings, payroll remittances, and year-end T4 preparation.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout--tip">
          <strong>Related reading</strong>
          Our <a href="/payroll-compliance-canada">Payroll Compliance in Canada guide</a> and
          <a href="/resources/year-end-bookkeeping-checklist-canada">Year-End Bookkeeping Checklist</a>
          walk through exactly what needs to be in order — and when.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 5 Divider ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="sign-5-heading">
    <h2 id="sign-5-heading">5. You're Making Decisions on Gut Feel, Not Data</h2>
    <p>
      This is usually the sign that matters most, because it's the one that limits growth
      directly. Spreadsheets don't naturally show trends, forecast forward, or flag when
      something looks off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Sign 5 Content ────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="content-section bg-subtle" aria-label="Sign 5 detail">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feature-block">
        <h3 class="feature-block-heading">Instinct Instead of Insight</h3>
        <p class="feature-block-body">
          Without dashboards or reporting built for the purpose, business owners end up relying
          on instinct — "I think we're doing okay this quarter" — instead of a clear answer
          backed by numbers. The businesses that scale smoothly are usually the ones that can
          answer key questions in minutes, not days.
        </p>
        <ul class="criteria-list">
          <li>
            <strong>"What's our margin trending toward?"</strong>
            <p>Spreadsheets show you a number. Purpose-built software shows you whether that number is moving in the right direction — and flags when it isn't.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"Can we afford to hire right now?"</strong>
            <p>Answering this from a spreadsheet means manually pulling cash flow, payroll projections, and pipeline data. A live dashboard gives you the answer immediately.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"Which clients or projects are most profitable?"</strong>
            <p>Without built-in reporting, profitability analysis requires hours of manual work — which means it usually doesn't happen at all.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Comparison Table Divider ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="b4s2" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
    <h2 id="comparison-heading">Spreadsheets vs. Accounting Software</h2>
    <p>
      Here's how the two approaches compare across the areas that matter most as a business grows.
    </p>
  </section>

